--TEST--
The compiler acts on an attribute only when its name resolves to one of its closed `Core`-owned list, so a whole name and the short name a `use` binds are one attribute, a userland name of the same shape is inert whatever it is spelled, and recognition leaves the payload object in the structural roster (`rule:core-classes/derive-attribute` and `rule:attributes/inert-metadata`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/the-names-the-compiler-acts-on
// `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`: the match is nominal and happens after resolution, so
// this case asks the same question from both sides in one program. The two
// spellings of `Core\Json\Derive` each have to reach the codec, and the two
// userland attributes each have to fail to, and a build that started matching
// by shape passes the first half while failing the second.
use Core\Json\Derive;

type Command = {name: string, note: string};
type Codec = {};

// The `use`d short name.
#[Derive]
class Short {
    public string $who;
    public function constructor(string $who) { $this->who = $who; }
}

// The same attribute, written whole.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Long {
    public string $who;
    public function constructor(string $who) { $this->who = $who; }
}

// A userland name whose payload is the empty shape `#[Core\Json\Derive]`'s own
// payload has. Shape is what retrieval matches on and is not what this matches
// on, so no codec is generated and `encode` refuses the instance at run time.
#[Codec]
class Plain {
    public string $who;
    public function constructor(string $who) { $this->who = $who; }
}

// The bare form resolves to no name at all, so it is the same refusal reached
// with nothing to spell.
#[{}]
class Bare {
    public string $who;
    public function constructor(string $who) { $this->who = $who; }
}

class Jobs {
    // One command called `import`. A second recognized `#[Command]` claiming
    // that word does not compile, so the userland twin below compiling is what
    // says it never reached the table.
    #[Core\Command(name: "import", about: "Read rows from a file")]
    public static function real(): void {}

    #[Command(name: "import", note: "mine")]
    public static function mine(): void {}
}

// Both spellings reach the generated codec.
echo "short: ", Core\Json::encode(new Short("ada")), "\n";
echo "long: ", Core\Json::encode(new Long("bea")), "\n";

// `rule:attributes/inert-metadata`: everything below is counted rather than read off a line
// each, so an attribute that crossed the line in either direction fails here
// instead of printing one plausible line of seven.
uint $agreed = 0;

// The userland name and the bare anonymous object are both inert, so neither class has
// an encoding.
try {
    echo Core\Json::encode(new Plain("cyd")), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

try {
    echo Core\Json::encode(new Bare("dot")), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

// The userland twin is ordinary metadata and reads back as itself.
?Command $mine = Core\Attributes::get<Command>(Jobs::mine(...));
if ($mine != null) {
    if ($mine->note == "mine") { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
}

// Recognition does not take a payload object out of the structural roster: the
// recognized `#[Core\Command]` payload, the userland twin and the `use`d
// `#[Derive]` are each one entry where they are attached.
if (Core\Arr::count(Core\Attributes::all<{name: string}>(Jobs::real(...))) == 1) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
if (Core\Arr::count(Core\Attributes::all<{}>(Jobs::mine(...))) == 1) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
if (Core\Arr::count(Core\Attributes::all<{}>(Short::constructor(...))) == 1) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
if (Core\Arr::count(Core\Attributes::all<{}>(Plain::constructor(...))) == 1) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }

echo "agreed: ", $agreed, " of 7\n";
--EXPECT--
short: {"who":"ada"}
long: {"who":"bea"}
agreed: 7 of 7
